\section{The observation contract}{


An enrollment states how the data records that a person was under
observation in a week. It is a flat key on the enrollment, \code{observed_var},
and it takes exactly one of two forms.

\preformatted{
observed_var: {column: rd_observed}      # a real logical person-week column
observed_var: {sentinel: row_presence}   # the skeleton is trimmed
}

The \code{row_presence} sentinel asserts that the caller already deleted every
unobserved person-week. A row then exists if and only if the person was
observed that week. Use it when the skeleton already deletes every
person-week the person was not under observation. The production skeleton
is one example. It deletes every person-week up to and including first
immigration, every person-week on or after emigration, and every
person-week after death. It keeps the death week itself. A real \code{observed}
column there would hold \code{TRUE} on every retained row. It could not
represent an absent week. Row presence as a silent proxy stays forbidden.
The sentinel is what makes the assumption explicit and testable.

Two flat sibling keys carry the arm tolerances:
\code{intervention_tolerance_weeks} and \code{comparator_tolerance_weeks}. Each MUST
be a whole number of at least 0. Each defaults to 0.

Every enrollment MUST declare \code{observed_var}. There is no exemption for an
older spec. A spec that cannot say who was under observation carries the
immortal-time defect silently. It looks exactly like a spec that can.
To migrate a spec, copy it to a new version and add the key to every
enrollment. Never edit a released spec version: that version is the record
of what produced a run.

The function rejects a declaration that gives both \code{column} and \code{sentinel},
a declaration that gives neither, and a sentinel name swereg does not know.
It cannot check that a named column exists and is logical, because it reads
no data. \code{\link[=tteplan_validate_spec]{tteplan_validate_spec()}} runs that check against the skeleton.
}

\section{The sparse_rule}{


A spec MAY declare one \code{sparse_rule} block. It turns a rare baseline
condition into an exclusion criterion of the whole study.

\preformatted{
sparse_rule:
  min_n: 100
  decided_by: ["03"]
  candidates: [rd_no_osd_i63_everbefore]
}
\itemize{
\item \code{min_n} MUST be one whole number from 1 to 2147483647, the largest R
integer.
\item \code{decided_by} MUST name one or more enrollments of the spec. Quote every
id: YAML reads an unquoted \code{01} as the integer 1, and the parser refuses
it.
\item Each entry of \code{candidates} MUST be the \code{implementation$variable} of a
confounder with \code{computed: true}.
}

s1 counts the persons who hold each candidate condition at baseline, per
arm, in each driving enrollment. A person counts once per arm when any of
her eligible person-trials in that arm holds the condition at its
recruiting week. A condition whose smallest count is below \code{min_n} is
excluded in every enrollment, and leaves every confounder set. Exclusion is
per person-trial: s1 drops a person-trial when the condition holds at that
trial's own recruiting week. Every other candidate stays a confounder. The
rule is decided from baseline counts in the eligible population, before the
comparator draw and without follow-up outcomes. \link{TTEPlan} stores the
decision in its \code{sparse_decision} field.
}

\section{The horizon_rule}{


Every spec MUST declare one \code{horizon_rule} block. It holds one key.

\preformatted{
horizon_rule:
  min_expected_events: 5
}

\code{min_expected_events} is the minimum number of expected events per arm in
each follow-up year. It MUST be one finite number of at least 0. The
function refuses a missing block, an empty block and any other key in the
block. \verb{$reload_spec()} copies a changed value onto a cached plan, because
the value does not change who is enrolled or how swereg weights them.
}

